===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[post-]][[traumatic]] [[stress]] [[disorder]]|-}}

# {{lb|en|psychology}} A [[psychological]] [[condition]] that develops following some [[stressful]] [[situation]] or [[event]], with symptoms such as [[sleep]] [[disturbance]], [[recurrent]] [[dream]]s, [[flashback]]s, [[hypervigilance]], [[depression]], [[dissociation]], [[withdrawal]] or [[lack]] of [[concentration]].
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2009
|last=Harris
|first=Kamala
|authorlink=Kamala Harris
|chapter=Putting Humpty Dumpty Back Together Again
|title={{w|Smart on Crime}}
|url=https://archive.org/details/smartoncrimecare00harr/
|location=[[San Francisco]]
|publisher={{w|Chronicle Books}}
|ISBN=978-0-8118-6528-9
|OCLC=191927227
|page=107
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/smartoncrimecare00harr/page/107/
|text=The evidence says that enormous numbers of children who live in households in high-crime neighborhoods are experiencing the psychological equivalent of battlefield conditions.[...]Studies show that many children who live under these conditions, 30 percent or more, are developing '''post-traumatic stress disorder''', or PTSD, similar to what we used to call “shell shock” in soldiers returning from war.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Cristina Criddle|title=Facebook moderator: ‘Every day was a nightmare’|work=BBC News|date=2021-05-12|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/technology-57088382|passage=An example of the contract, read out in the committee said: “I understand that exposure to this content may give me '''post traumatic stress disorder'''. I will engage in a mandatory wellness coaching session but I understand that those are not conditions and may not be sufficient to prevent my contracting PTSD.”}}

